Why Testing is Necessary (ISTQB Style)
1. Identify Defects Early
Concept: Testing helps uncover errors before software reaches users.

Example: A banking app tested during development reveals calculation errors in interest rates before release.

2. Ensure Quality & Reliability
Concept: Testing validates that the product meets requirements and performs consistently.

Example: An e-commerce site tested for checkout flow ensures customers can place orders without failures.

3. Prevent Failures in Production
Concept: Testing reduces risk of costly failures after deployment.

Example: A healthcare system tested for data accuracy avoids incorrect patient records in hospitals.

4. Verify Requirements
Concept: Testing confirms the product matches business/user needs.

Example: Requirement: “User must reset password via email.” Testing ensures reset links are sent correctly and expire after 24 hours.

5. Support Maintenance & Upgrades
Concept: Regression testing ensures new changes don’t break existing features.

Example: Adding a “wishlist” feature to an app doesn’t affect login or payment functionality.

6. Build Customer Confidence
Concept: A well-tested product increases trust and satisfaction.

Example: A ride-hailing app tested for GPS accuracy reassures users about pickup/drop locations.

7. Legal & Safety Compliance
Concept: Testing ensures adherence to standards and regulations.

Example: Aviation software tested for safety compliance avoids life-threatening risks.

8. Cost Effectiveness
Concept: Fixing defects early is cheaper than fixing them post-release.

Example: A bug found during unit testing costs less to fix than one discovered by thousands of users in production.
